<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assinie</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Assinie is a coastal town in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a>, on the sandbar between the Aby lagoon and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>. It was an early trading place where Europeans exchanged guns, cloth and spirits for gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and the French briefly held a fort there in the early 18th century. A treaty with the Sanwi rulers in 1843 made it one of the first footholds of French Ivory Coast.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Assinie-Mafia lies about 80 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abidjan">Abidjan</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>, a long, narrow settlement on both sides of the outlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aby-lagoon">Aby Lagoon</a>, where the coast road from Abidjan ends. Opposite the town a narrow peninsula, 15 km long and 100 to 1,000 metres wide, runs from the west; its tip at the mouth of the lagoon is called La Passe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Assinie, formerly Issiny, was the first trading post on this coast, though nothing of that time survives. Five Capuchin missionaries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a> settled there in 1637 but soon left because of the climate and sickness. French traders and missionaries came in 1687, and on the orders of Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-du-casse">Jean-Baptiste du Casse</a>, director of the Compagnie du Sénégal, the chevalier d'Amon set up a settlement for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a>. D'Amon took two young men to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Aniaba, said to be a prince, and the interpreter Banga; they were presented to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. The priest Godefroy Loyer, sent there in 1701, published an account of the kingdom of Issiny in 1714.</p><p>By 1850 the French had a fortified trading post at Assinie, which an inspection that year found staffed by 40 people, five of them Europeans. Yellow fever and British competition slowed French expansion: in 1857, of 50 Europeans at the posts of Assinie, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-bassam">Grand-Bassam</a> and Dabou, 32 died. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-verdier">Arthur Verdier</a> developed the region from 1870; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa were planted from 1881 and logging began in 1885. In 1890 Marcel Treich-Laplène moved the first French school, founded at Elima in 1887, to Assinie-Mafia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Assinie was the third largest port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> in 1907 but soon lost its trade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-bassam">Grand-Bassam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bingerville">Bingerville</a> and finally Port-Bouët and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abidjan">Abidjan</a>. It is now a resort favoured by people from Abidjan and a sub-prefecture of Adiaké Department, which had 16,721 inhabitants in eight villages in 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Assinie-Mafia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>France signed treaties at Grand-Bassam and <b>Assinie</b> in 1843, and merchants such as Arthur Verdier kept a French presence.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-ivory-coast">French Ivory Coast</a></li><li><blockquote>Under their leader Amon Ndoufou I they settled around Krinjabo and the Aby lagoon, subjecting the Eotile and Essouma peoples of the coast, including those of the trading town of <b>Assinie</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sanwi">Kingdom of Sanwi</a></li></ul></section>
